<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yigael Yadin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1917">1917</a>–<a href="/years/1984">1984</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yigael Yadin was an Israeli archaeologist, soldier and politician. Chief of staff of the Israel Defense Forces from 1949 to 1952, he then led major excavations at Hazor in 1955–1958 and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masada">Masada</a> in 1963–1965 and published studies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dead-sea-scrolls">Dead Sea Scrolls</a>. He later served as deputy prime minister of Israel from 1977 to 1981.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yigael-yadin.jpg" alt="Yigael Yadin" width="320" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: IDF Spokesperson's Unit photographer · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yigael_Yadin,_Chief_of_General_Staff.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yigael Yadin was born Yigael Sukenik in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> on 20 March 1917, when the city was part of Ottoman Palestine. His father was the Polish-Jewish archaeologist Eleazar Sukenik, and his mother, Hasya Sukenik-Feinsold, was a teacher and women's rights activist. He joined the Haganah at the age of 15. In 1946 he left it after an argument with its commander, Yitzhak Sadeh, over whether a machine gun should be standard squad equipment.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1948 Yadin interrupted his university studies to return to active service and was Israel's Head of Operations in the 1948 Arab–Israeli War, responsible for many of its key decisions. In April he oversaw the secret biological warfare operation Cast Thy Bread, a campaign to poison the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> of Palestinian villages with bacteria and prevent the return of the evicted, probably with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-ben-gurion">David Ben-Gurion</a>'s authorisation. He became Chief of Staff of the Israel Defense Forces on 9 November 1949 and resigned on 7 December 1952 over Ben-Gurion's cuts to the military budget.</p><p>He then turned to archaeology. In 1956 he received the Israel Prize in Jewish studies for his doctoral thesis on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dead-sea-scrolls">Dead Sea Scrolls</a>: his father had bought three of the seven scrolls found in 1947, and Yadin bought the other four in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> in 1954. He excavated the Qumran Caves, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masada">Masada</a>, Hazor, Tel Megiddo and caves in the Judean Desert where objects of the Bar Kokhba revolt were found, and he thought the Solomonic Gate at Tel Gezer the high point of his career.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Yadin was military adviser to Prime Minister Levi Eshkol on the eve of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/six-day-war">Six-Day War</a>, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yom-kippur-war">Yom Kippur War</a> he sat on the Agranat Commission. In 1976 he founded the Democratic Movement for Change, known as Dash, in response to alleged corruption in the Labor Alignment. In the elections of 1977 Dash won 15 of the 120 seats in the Knesset and joined Menachem Begin's coalition after a few months.</p><p>As Deputy Prime Minister he played an important part in the contacts with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> that led to the Camp David Accords and the peace treaty. Dash broke into splinter factions, and he sat as an independent for the rest of his term. He retired from politics in 1981, and died on 28 June 1984 of a heart attack.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yigael Yadin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From Cave 3 came the Copper Scroll, and from Cave 11 the Temple Scroll, which <b>Yigael Yadin</b> called &quot;The Torah According to the Essenes&quot; and Hartmut Stegemann thought of no exceptional significance.</blockquote><a href="/work/dead-sea-scrolls">Dead Sea Scrolls · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>Excavations by <b>Yigael Yadin</b> and Amnon Ben-Tor uncovered statues and cuneiform tablets.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-hazor">Kingdom of Hazor</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Jerusalem, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. Died in Jerusalem, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.78,35.23&amp;year=1960" aria-label="Jerusalem on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Yigael Yadin"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5380 4839h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5380 4839h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.78,35.23&amp;year=1960">What was here in 1960? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
